# FACT · a virtual type is a word honored as a type without an m/t taxonomy slot (2026-08-08)

THE PRESS, verbatim (Q132's ruling, correcting what he called a key misunderstanding): "a virtual
type simply means a word that we honor as it was a word we know how to effect as a type within our
taxonomy even though it does not appear as a taxnomony. LIST is the principle example provided with
the system of actually doing that." (his spelling kept)

WHAT IT MEANS: not every word the shop treats as a type needs an m/t row/facet. A virtual type is
effected -- given real behavior, recognized, actually usable as a type -- through some OTHER
mechanism entirely, and m/t simply never gains a slot for it. Taxonomy-completeness questions were
checking the wrong ledger for these words.

THE EXAMPLE, confirmed on disk: LIST. m/t's L row reads LAYOUT LIBRARY LINK LICENSE -- four facets,
unchanged, no LIST, none due. LIST is instead effected via l/l (minted CANDIDATE, Q249, 2026-08-06):
a delimiter-to-SHA1 registry -- ( ) [ ] { } < > each pinned to its own hash -- the "dedicated-
register list-encapsulation protocol from delimiter baselines" that IS list-typing, with no taxonomy
word anywhere in the loop.

WHAT THIS CLOSES: Q132 -- L does not gain LIST; the row stays four; the question's own premise
(every effected type needs a taxonomy slot) was the misunderstanding.

WHAT STAYS OPEN: l/l itself is still CANDIDATE, not promoted by this ruling -- the GENERAL principle
(virtual types exist; m/t is not the only place a word becomes real) is what landed here, not a
grant of settled status to any one instance. Worth a look, not yet asked: whether other words
proposed for m/t slots (Q139's open registers among them) are actually virtual types in disguise --
flagged, not claimed.
